# AI in this course — what to expect

Short guide for **students** in UNCG ILRS / MSIA courses. Your published **course-handbook and Canvas** win if anything here differs for a specific term.

## Preamble

**AI** has become a part of our lives and while policy around it, or its usage, may change, that genie will not go back into the bottle.
As a student preparing for a career in data, or as a student who is already working in this space and wanting to gain new skills, knowledge
of AI and how to best **leverage it as a tool** is critical.

This is the **org default**. Individual courses may tighten or rewrite it — especially courses whose subject *is* AI systems. Always read this file in *this* repository.

## What AI is for here

You may use AI tools to **support learning**, for example:

- Explaining a concept in different words
- Helping to work through a concept or task that you're struggling with (think of it as a _tutor_)
- Helping debug **your** code or notebook (with sensitive data removed)
- Brainstorming approaches or study questions
- Checking your work against a rubric *after* you have attempted it

You may use AI tools to **support productivity**, for example:

- Prompting an LLM to generate a specific scaffold or document layout to best convey/portray the information (please consider submitting, at least, your initial prompt)
- Prompting the LLM to refactor your code or update the contracts to down-stream functions based on upstream changes.

**DO NOT OUTSOURCE YOUR CRITICAL THINKING**. AI is **not** a substitute for understanding the material, methods, or ethical constraints of the assignment.

## What remains your responsibility

- **You own everything submitted** and you must be able to defend and explain various decisions if asked.
- Graded submissions should reflect **your** reasoning, decisions, and wording unless an assignment explicitly allows more.
- If you cannot explain or re-create what you submitted, you are not ready to turn it in.
- You are responsible for factual errors, unsafe recommendations, and policy violations in anything you submit—even if a tool suggested them.

## Allowed vs careful vs not OK (defaults)

| Usually OK | Be careful | Not OK (defaults) |
|:--|:--|:--|
| Concept questions, definitions | Large paste of your draft for “rewrite it all” | Submitting AI-generated work as solely yours when the goal is *your* skill |
| Explaining an error message | Using AI on data that is not clearly public | Putting **restricted** data in consumer AI tools (see below) |
| Practice problems you invent | Relying on AI for literature claims without checking sources | Uploading classmates’ work, answer keys, or private course materials |
| Rubric self-check after your attempt | — | Ignoring a stricter rule on *this* course’s course-handbook |

## Data, privacy, and restricted sources

- Do **not** paste PHI, real student records, or **credentialed** extracts into ChatGPT, Claude, Copilot, or similar tools unless the instructor has named an **approved** path.
- Prefer **public sample files** from the course repo (e.g. under `data/public/`) when trying tools.
- Credentials, API keys, and login details never go into chats.

If you are unsure whether a file is allowed, **ask the instructor before** using an external AI tool on it.

## Honesty and disclosure

- If the course-handbook or assignment asks you to **disclose** AI use, do so briefly (what tool, what for). A Canvas checkbox or one sentence is enough when that is what is requested.
- Fabricating sources, results, or “I ran this” when you did not is an integrity issue—with or without AI—and only harms your understanding.

## How this relates to coding agents and project files

Some courses may include files such as `AGENTS.md` or skill folders for **optional** coding agents (Codex, Claude Code, Grok, etc.). Those files guide the *tools*. They do not replace this page or the course-handbook.

You are not required to use local agents unless the course says so. Browser-based assistants are fine when they fit the rules above.

## When policies conflict

1. **course-handbook / Canvas for your term**
2. This document (this repository’s copy)
3. Informal advice from tools or classmates

## Questions

Ask the instructor or TA early—especially about data and disclosure—before a deadline makes the answer urgent.

